/* ============================================================================
   geometric-pmf.epher -- waiting time for the first success, by formula

   Roll a die until the first 6 appears. The rolls are independent, so
   the first 6 lands on roll k exactly when rolls 1..k - 1 show 1..5 and
   roll k shows 6:

     P(T = k) = (1 - p)^(k - 1) * p,    p = 1/6 for a die,

   the geometric distribution. There is no geometric builtin in epher, so
   the script evaluates the formula itself; the probabilities of a fair
   die are exact fractions (1/6, 5/36, 25/216, ...) until the denominator
   outgrows 1000 and epher switches to decimals.

   The probabilities over all k sum to 1, but only in the limit: after
   10 rolls there is still a (5/6)^10 = 0.1615 chance that no 6 has come,
   so P(T <= 10) = 0.8385. With a fair coin (p = 1/2) the tail after 10
   tosses is 2^-10 and the partial sum 1023/1024 is already close to 1.

   Source: Ross, "A First Course in Probability", chapter 4.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/probability/distributions/geometric-pmf.epher"
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- helpers ----
def pgeo(k, p) = (1 - p) ^ (k - 1) * p   // P(first success on trial k)

// ---- case 1: waiting for a 6 on a fair die, p = 1/6 ----
for k in 1 to 5 do pgeo(k, 1/6)
print("P(T <= 10):", 1 - (5/6) ^ 10)
print("P(T > 10), the tail:", (5/6) ^ 10)

/* ---- case 2: a fair coin, p = 1/2, and the partial sum to 10 ----
   The sum of the point probabilities from k = 1 to 10, accumulated in a
   packed while loop (guide 1.30), is 1023/1024 = 0.9990234375, close to
   1 because the tail after 10 tosses is only 2^-10. */
v = {1, 0}
while v[1] <= 10 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + pgeo(v[1], 1/2)}
print("sum of P(T = 1..10), p = 1/2:", v[2])
print("exact: 1023/1024 =", 1023 / 1024)
/* ---- expected output ----
= {1/6, 5/36, 25/216, 0.096450617284, 0.0803755144033}
= P(T <= 10): 0.83849441711
= P(T > 10), the tail: 0.16150558289
= {1, 0}
= sum of P(T = 1..10), p = 1/2: 0.9990234375
= exact: 1023/1024 = 0.9990234375
*/
